PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A}

The trp leader transcript contains four regions that can pair as alternative stem-loops. Under tryptophan-replete conditions the ribosome translates TrpL to its stop codon and thereby sequesters region 2; regions 3 and 4 are therefore free to form the terminator hairpin that aborts transcription. A mutation that prevents region 1 from pairing with region 2 leaves region 2 free to pair with region 3 as soon as region 3 is transcribed. Formation of the 2-3 antiterminator precludes formation of the 3-4 terminator, so transcription continues even when tryptophan is high.  

Options B, C, D and E either favor terminator formation, alter termination efficiency without blocking stem-loop formation, or reduce transcription initiation altogether; none of them prevents the 3-4 hairpin. Thus A is the only mutation that directly favors the antiterminator and thereby blocks terminator formation under repressing conditions.}

