2.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A}

The trp leader RNA forms mutually exclusive hairpins: 1-2 (pause), 2-3 (antiterminator), or 3-4 (terminator). High tryptophan allows the ribosome to translate the entire leader peptide, covering region 2 until the stop codon; this frees region 3 to pair with region 4 and form the terminator.  

A mutation that blocks region 1 from pairing with region 2 leaves region 2 available after ribosome release. As soon as region 3 is transcribed, region 2 pairs with it to create the 2-3 antiterminator, which sequesters region 3 and prevents 3-4 formation. Transcription therefore continues even under tryptophan-replete conditions.  

B would block antiterminator formation and favor termination; C strengthens the terminator; D and E alter expression level or initiation without changing hairpin choice. Only A directly precludes the 3-4 terminator under repressing conditions.}

